Epic: Compass AI Copilot (MCP-based navigation, data and page-aware assistant)
Summary

Build an MCP server that lets users of Compass ask questions in natural language and get answers or actions in return: navigating the app, fetching data from Compass APIs for trend and comparison analysis, and explaining or analysing what is currently on screen. Build it as reusable platform components so other projects can adopt it with configuration only.

Background / Problem Statement

Compass users currently have to find the right page, apply filters, read charts and work out insights themselves. [Add pain points: e.g. a steep learning curve, time spent on monthly reviews, repeated support questions.]

Goals
Let users reach any Compass page by intent (“take me to the regional sales breakdown”).
Let users get data-backed answers: past-month trends, month-to-date vs the previous period, period comparisons.
Let users get explanations and analysis of the content they are currently viewing.
Deliver the capability as generic, versioned components reusable across projects, with Compass as the first adopter.
Non-Goals (v1)
Write operations (create, update or delete) in Compass.
Autonomous multi-step actions without user confirmation.
Support for projects other than Compass (the design must allow it, but onboarding is a later phase).
Compass-Specific Requirements
Navigation: Expose all in-scope Compass routes with descriptions, parameters and example intents. Navigation is allowlisted and driven by config.
Data access: Integrate with Compass APIs [list: API names / OpenAPI specs] using the logged-in user’s token, so users only see data they are entitled to.
Metrics: Define the Compass metric catalog [list key metrics] with definitions, synonyms, granularity and “good direction” (up or down).
Page awareness: Each key Compass page [list pages] provides structured context (filters, selected entities, table and chart data) to the assistant.
Analysis: Trend (last N months), period comparison, and current-month status with a projected month-end, all computed in code, with the LLM only narrating.
Channel: [Embedded Compass chat widget / external MCP client]
Reusable Components (platform layer)
Component	Responsibility	Project-specific input
mcp-core	Server bootstrap, auth passthrough, session handling, logging, caching, module registry	None
mcp-manifest	Loads and validates the project manifest (routes, APIs, metrics)	Manifest file
mcp-nav	list_destinations, navigate_to, get_current_location	Route definitions
mcp-browser-bridge	JS SDK embedded in the site; WebSocket/SSE link to the server; executes navigation and returns page context	Embed and context providers
mcp-page-context	Context-provider registry plus a DOM/accessibility-tree fallback, with PII redaction	Per-page providers
mcp-api-connector	Generates tools from OpenAPI specs; handles auth forwarding, retries and rate limits	API specs
mcp-metrics	Metric catalog and list_metrics, query_metric	Metric definitions
mcp-analytics	get_trend, compare_periods, current_month_status, with anomaly detection	None (driven by the metric catalog)
mcp-prompts-resources	Reusable prompts (“explain this page”, “monthly review”), site map and glossary resources	Glossary text
mcp-security	Prompt-injection guards, allowlists, read-only enforcement, audit log	Policy config
Child Stories

Story 1: Core server and manifest loader
Set up the MCP server (TypeScript, official MCP SDK), a module registration interface and the manifest schema with startup validation.
AC: The server starts only with a valid manifest; invalid configs fail with clear errors; the manifest schema is versioned.

Story 2: Navigation module
Implement list_destinations, navigate_to and get_current_location for Compass routes.
AC: Only manifest-listed routes are navigable; route parameters are validated; unknown intents return suggestions rather than errors.

Story 3: Browser bridge SDK
Build the embeddable script with a secure channel to the server, navigation execution and a heartbeat/reconnect.
AC: The bridge authenticates per user session; navigation commands execute within 1s; it works in Compass’s supported browsers.

Story 4: Page context providers
Create the provider registry and implement providers for [top N Compass pages], with a DOM fallback.
AC: get_page_context returns the URL, filters and structured table/chart data; PII fields are redacted; page text is tagged as untrusted data.

Story 5: API connector and metric catalog
Ingest Compass OpenAPI specs, define the metric catalog and implement list_metrics and query_metric.
AC: Calls use the user’s forwarded token; read-only by default; metric synonyms resolve (“sales” → “revenue”).

Story 6: Analysis module
Implement get_trend, compare_periods and current_month_status.
AC: Calculations are deterministic and unit-tested; outputs include the series, absolute and percent change, and flagged anomalies; edge cases (partial months, missing data) are handled.

Story 7: Prompts and resources
Add the site map, metric glossary and prompt templates.
AC: The assistant answers “what does this page show?” using the glossary definitions.

Story 8: Security, observability and guardrails
Add injection guards, an audit log of tool calls, rate limiting and write-confirmation hooks.
AC: Page content cannot trigger tool calls on its own; every tool call is logged with user, tool and parameters; PII does not reach logs.

Story 9: Packaging and documentation
Split into versioned packages, publish to the internal registry, and write an onboarding guide.
AC: A sample project can be onboarded using only a manifest and the bridge embed, in under [X] days.

Story 10: Evaluation and rollout
Build a test set of real user intents and track accuracy; run a pilot with [user group].
AC: Intent-to-tool accuracy ≥ [X]%; analysis outputs verified against the source data; pilot feedback captured.

Non-Functional Requirements
Response latency: [e.g. p95 < 5s for analysis queries]
Security review and penetration test before release
Data privacy: no storage of page content beyond the session [confirm with policy]
Observability: dashboards for tool usage, errors and latency
